Z||000021||rogergarrison||08-18-2001||12:51 AM||||Mark needs to retire. hasnt done anything but drive in circles for a lonngggg time. He looks like hes 75 too.||||reg||1
Z||000022||Songman||08-18-2001||12:52 AM||||Oh! That is a good one! The white hair goes great with the white uniform doesn't it! <P>Still can't get used to DJ without his moustache!<P>Oh! And Roger, I just wanted to respond to one more thing about your earlier post. They do still require so many street cars to be able to use the car on the track any given year. But the rule only applies to body shape, not running gear. That's why I bought my 87 Aerocoupe. They had to make so many units with that big back window to be able to put them on the track. And I can guarantee that the little stock 305HO that is in my car never came close to the track!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif">  <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A><p>[This message has been edited by Songman (edited 08-18-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000023||maryjane||08-18-2001||02:04 AM||||Yeah, it's a sport as much as anything<BR>else nowdays(did my monitor shrink or are<BR>these replies r  e  a  l  y  w i d e ?)<BR>I've never done any of it but I would <BR>imagine it takes pretty good stamina and<BR>at least some muscles to fight the G forces on the turns. Ever ride a really fast amusment park ride and try to keep from squashing the person sitting on<BR> the outside of the turn?<BR>Imagine doing that for hours. I know the harness helps, but it's still got to be a strain.||||reg||1
Z||000024||Monkeyman||08-18-2001||03:43 AM||||Little E's victory was fixed, NASCAR is a sport, and <B>JEFF GORDON RULES !! !! !!</B>.<P>------------------<BR>'Consciousness:  That annoying time between naps.'<P>MM||||reg||1
Z||000025||TRiAD||08-18-2001||09:09 AM||||I'm more into road rafing myself. I think if NASCAR gave the manufacturers and teams 3 years' notice that they were going to change the rules, and went to STOCK cars, the manufacturers would have to come up with FR V8 cars for all of us (2 thumbs up!) and the cars on the track wouldn't be circling billboards anymore. Yeah, they'd still be plastered with logos, but at least they'd be a real car! THIS would be interesting! Oh, wait...there's several racing series kinda' like that...I'll go watch <I>them</I>. <P>Here's a quote from a team owner recently when HE was asked this question...<P><I>"First and foremost; it's a show."</I><P>That's all I have to say about that.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000026||Songman||08-18-2001||10:02 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Monkeyman:<BR><B>Little E's victory was fixed, NASCAR is a sport, and [b]JEFF GORDON RULES !! !! !!</B>.<BR>[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Oh man! Just fell out of my chair! heheh<BR>But I like you anyway...<P>Joking aside though.. How do you think they convinced those teams and sponsors that it was worth all of the thousands of dollars, and if some cases much more, that they would lose just to be so sentimental? Not all of those teams are rolling in money, you know. Plus, if racing is all about making money as this thread implies, it just doesn't make sense that they would do that... Little E has not been the consistent threat that everyone thought he was going to be. I'll admit that. But he has had his days when he was untouchable. This just happened to be one of those days. And he had a little bit of a reason to try harder this day.<P>It is no secret that Dale Jr was shaken up after his father's death. He didn't drive as hard as he did before and he had a similar wreck to the one that killed his father not to long after. But Jr found himself at the controls all of a sudden, just in the wink of an eye it was all on him. Can you imagine what that must have been like? An Daytona was a track that had denied his father many times and finally took him. Dale Jr was a man on a mission on his next trip back there. And the team was behind him in his quest. They won that race fair and square... Gordon was trying to catch him and couldn't.<P>I used to say that I was a Dale fan because of my name. DJ, Big E, and Little E. Jr let me down with his attitude after he came into the big time so this is not an attempt to take up for him. This is just the ramblings of a race fan that wishes people would stop trying to pull apart a tradition that makes millions of people happy every week. Hundreds of thousands live at each track and millions more on TV. And honestly, the so-called big three sports in this country would be much easier to rig, and each has a history or doing so. Plus, race drivers have never gone on strike and left their fans high and dry. NASCAR is still a sport of and for real people. There is no pretention at a race track. Sure, trailer parks all over the country empty on race day. They save their money all year to be able to afford to go to whatever races they can. But it is just not those low-brow rednecks that a lot of people on this board loves to look down on. <P>I just had a funny thought pass through my head! Most of the things that I love have always been considered to be for backwater rednecks... NASCAR, Country Music, Harleys, and old hot rods. Well, I guess I'll say it again... I'm a redneck and proud of it!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/icons/icon7.gif"> <P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||7
Z||000027||bHooper||08-18-2001||10:22 AM||||as i slip into my asbestos suit.... (i dont watch nascar, but here's my opinion anyway)<P>personally, i believe nascar is a talentless series with a few talented drivers.  tony stewart and jeff gordon are some of the few drivers that actually were brought up through proper racing chanels.<P>nascar does take some physical ability, but doesn't require the skill that say... sportscar racing, CART or F1 require (road racing in general).  because in these forms of the sport, you have other variables thrown in... really high speads, change of direction (thats a big one), often times fenders arent available, and the weather can be an issue (something most nascar drivers have never witnessed).<P>granted, occaisionally, a nascar driver can cross over with some success (earnhart at the 24 hours of daytona, this year), but generally not. <P>also, i really lost respect for nascar (as  a skilled series), when i was at my parents watching a race (my dad had it on, i had no choice), and the in car cam showed one driver  smoking a marlboro (while driving) and another was eating a big mac!<P>i guess it does take some talent to be able to eat and turn left at 175 mph!<P>(this is not meant as a flame, just one persons thoughts on the subject)<P><P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><FONT COLOR="red"><B>Red</B></FONT></FONT> 86 GT 5 speed<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><B>Black</B></FONT> <u>86 SE Automatic</u>||||reg||1
Z||000028||Songman||08-18-2001||10:45 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by bHooper:<BR><B>nascar does take some physical ability, but doesn't require the skill that say... sportscar racing, CART or F1 require (road racing in general).  because in these forms of the sport, you have other variables thrown in... really high speads, change of direction (thats a big one), often times fenders arent available, and the weather can be an issue (something most nascar drivers have never witnessed).<P>granted, occaisionally, a nascar driver can cross over with some success (earnhart at the 24 hours of daytona, this year), but generally not. <P>also, i really lost respect for nascar (as  a skilled series), when i was at my parents watching a race (my dad had it on, i had no choice), and the in car cam showed one driver  smoking a marlboro (while driving) and another was eating a big mac!<P>i guess it does take some talent to be able to eat and turn left at 175 mph!<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hey Hoop! Those were the commercials everyone has been talking about! hehe Trust me, there is no one smoking or eating in a NASCAR race car. They can get a quick drink during a pit stop but that is it...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"><P>And don't forget that NASCAR does road road courses too... As much as I hate to say it, it is tough to beat Jeff Gordon on a road course. But also don't forget that the NASCAR tracks are high banked and believe me it take skill to drive through those turns at 200mph. On a couple of tracks you can just barely walk up the banks they are so steep. Those guys are having to fight those cars every inch through those turns and are doing it with 43 other guys all within inches of each other.<P>I recommend everyone go rent Days of Thunder. The movie is a lot of Hollywood for sure and NASCAR would never have allowed what happened in the movie, but the lessons are there. A driver from another series comes to NASCAR and basically has to learn everything new. The explanation was that in open wheels his car weighed half as much and his tires were twice as wide. And in stock cars his car weighed twice as much and his tires were half as wide.<P>I agree that NASCAR is not perfect. I would love to see restrictor plates gone and the old days of "run what you brung" brought back but it is not going to happen because of a few reason... One is that too many people would die. Two is that if that happened you would only see the teams that could afford it crossing the line first every week. NASCAR is competition. And I'm sure all of these other series that you all do like have rules of the same sort. And regardless of what you think of the sport, and yes it is still a sport, don't take anything away from those guys who come out there every week and risk life and limb. They are there because they love it and they all worked very hard to get there.<P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||7
Z||000029||fogglethorpe||08-18-2001||12:27 PM||||I don't know if it's a sport, but I do know that Purdue is its favorite brand of chicken.<P>......................<BR>People in glass houses are either exhibitionists, or spend a mint on draperies.||||reg||1
